NodeDR POS for Debian/Ubuntu
============================

This package installs NodeDR POS as a native system service. Docker is not
used and is not required; the Docker Compose deployment described in the
project README remains available and is unaffected by this package.

Quick start
-----------
After installation the service is already running. Open

    http://localhost:1994

or launch "NodeDR POS" from the applications menu, and create the admin
account. From other devices on the same network use the till's IP address,
for example http://192.168.1.20:1994.

Layout
------
  /opt/nodedr-pos/            application, bundled Node.js runtime
  /etc/nodedr-pos/            configuration (preserved across upgrades)
  /var/lib/nodedr-pos/        SQLite database, session secret, backups
  /var/cache/nodedr-pos/      Next.js runtime cache (safe to delete)
  /usr/bin/nodedr-pos         operator command-line tool

Service management
------------------
There is one unit to operate:

    sudo systemctl status nodedr-pos
    sudo systemctl restart nodedr-pos
    sudo systemctl stop nodedr-pos

It supervises two processes — nodedr-pos-backend.service (the API, which owns
the database and the printer) and nodedr-pos-frontend.service (the web
interface). They are separate units so that each is restarted, sandboxed and
diagnosed independently; stopping or restarting nodedr-pos propagates to both.

Convenience wrapper:

    nodedr-pos doctor       check ports, database and printer wiring
    nodedr-pos logs         follow the logs
    sudo nodedr-pos backup  online, crash-safe copy of the database
    nodedr-pos --help

Configuration
-------------
Edit /etc/nodedr-pos/nodedr-pos.conf, then restart the service. The file is a
dpkg conffile, so your changes survive upgrades and dpkg will ask before
overwriting it. The most common change is HOSTNAME=127.0.0.1 to restrict the
POS to the till itself.

Note that BACKEND_PORT cannot be changed after the fact: the web interface
resolves its /api proxy destination at build time. Rebuilding the package with
a different BACKEND_PORT is the supported way to move it.

Thermal printers
----------------
The service account is added to the "lp" group and udev rules ship in
/usr/lib/udev/rules.d/60-nodedr-pos-printer.rules, so a USB ESC/POS printer
works without further setup. Verify with:

    nodedr-pos doctor
    ls -l /dev/usb/lp0

then use Settings > Receipt > "USB printer check" in the app. If the printer
is bound by CUPS at the same time, stop the CUPS queue for it — the kernel
lets only one writer hold the device.

Browser printing and PDF download work with no printer configuration at all
and are the default.

Backups
-------
    sudo nodedr-pos backup                       # -> /var/lib/nodedr-pos/backups/
    sudo nodedr-pos restore /path/to/backup.db

`backup` uses SQLite's VACUUM INTO, so it is safe to run while the shop is
trading. Copying pos.db with cp while the service is running is not.

Removal
-------
    sudo apt remove nodedr-pos     keeps the database in /var/lib/nodedr-pos
    sudo apt purge  nodedr-pos     deletes the database and all shop data

Take a backup before purging. There is no undo.

Node.js
-------
The package bundles its own Node.js runtime in /opt/nodedr-pos/runtime and
does not depend on, install, or interfere with any system nodejs package.
This is deliberate: the application uses native modules (better-sqlite3, usb)
compiled against a specific Node ABI, and Debian and Ubuntu ship Node versions
older than the one this application requires.

